# Sakhi server — environment
# Copy to .env (or export these) and fill in what you need. Everything here is
# optional: with no keys at all the app still runs, using rules-only triage.
# That is a real degraded mode, not a broken one.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AI reasoning provider  — PICK ONE FREE OPTION (or none)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The app uses an AI layer for: triage compound-pattern reasoning, prescription
# OCR, and the plain-language report summary. With NO key set, it runs in
# rules-only mode (triage still works; OCR + AI summary are simply disabled).
#
# You do NOT need to pay anyone. Set ONE of the two free keys below and run
# `npm run test:llm` to confirm it works. Groq is the easiest (no credit card).
#
# LLM_PROVIDER picks which one to use. "auto" (default) uses whichever key is set,
# preferring groq, then gemini, then watsonx.
# LLM_PROVIDER=auto

# --- Groq (free, fastest, no credit card): https://console.groq.com/keys ------
GROQ_API_KEY=
# Model ids change on free tiers. If you ever get "model decommissioned", pick a
# current one from https://console.groq.com/docs/models and update these.
# GROQ_MODEL=openai/gpt-oss-20b
# GROQ_VISION_MODEL=meta-llama/llama-4-scout-17b-16e-instruct

# --- IBM Granite, FREE — two ways (great for an IBM hackathon story) ----------
# Granite is open-source, so you can run it free without paid watsonx.
#
# (a) Hugging Face free inference (IBM Granite in the cloud, just needs a token):
#     Get a token at https://huggingface.co/settings/tokens  (free, no card)
# HF_API_KEY=hf_...your token...
# HF_MODEL=ibm-granite/granite-3.3-8b-instruct     # change if a model 404s
#
# (b) Ollama — IBM Granite running LOCALLY (no key, no limits, offline).
#     OPTIONAL and installed SEPARATELY — NOT required to run Sakhi, and it is
#     used ONLY when you explicitly opt in with the LLM_PROVIDER line below.
#     One-time, on your own machine (the model is ~4.9 GB, downloaded by Ollama
#     into its own store — it is never part of this repo):
#         1) install Ollama:  https://ollama.com
#         2) pull the model:  ollama pull granite3.3
#     Then uncomment this ONE line to switch Sakhi to local Granite:
# LLM_PROVIDER=ollama
#     Optional overrides (these only CUSTOMISE Ollama; they do NOT turn it on):
# OLLAMA_MODEL=granite3.3
# OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1

# --- Google Gemini (free, best OCR vision): https://aistudio.google.com/apikey -
GEMINI_API_KEY=
# GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-2.0-flash-lite
# GEMINI_VISION_MODEL=gemini-2.0-flash-lite

# --- IBM watsonx (OPTIONAL — costs money beyond a tiny free tier; NOT default) -
# Only set these if you specifically have IBM credits to spend. Everything works
# without them. `npm run test:watson` checks these specifically.
# WATSONX_API_KEY=
# WATSONX_PROJECT_ID=
# WATSONX_URL=https://us-south.ml.cloud.ibm.com
# WATSONX_MODEL_ID=ibm/granite-3-8b-instruct

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Session storage (Supabase Postgres)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Leave both blank and sessions are kept in server/data/sessions.json. That is
# the zero-config demo path and it works — but Render's disk is ephemeral, so a
# restart loses the triage queue. Set these for any real deployment.
#
# Setup: create a project at supabase.com, then run migrations/001_schema.sql in
# the SQL editor. Both variables are required together; a URL without a key
# falls back to the JSON file and logs a warning at boot.
SUPABASE_URL=
# Project Settings -> API -> service_role. This key bypasses Row Level Security
# and is the only credential that can read the session table, so it is
# server-only: never put it in client/.env, never prefix it with VITE_, and
# rotate it if it is ever committed. The anon key is not used here — the browser
# never talks to Supabase directly.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PORT=4000
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173,http://127.0.0.1:5173
# Set only when running behind a reverse proxy you control, so that
# X-Forwarded-For can be trusted for rate limiting.
# TRUST_PROXY=false

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Demo accounts (development only)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Password used by `npm run seed:demo`.
# DEMO_PASSWORD=SakhiDemo@2026!

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Email (verification + password reset)  — Point 2
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Leave blank and emails print to the SERVER CONSOLE instead of being sent — a
# real dev/demo mode (copy the link from the terminal), not a broken one. Set
# these for real delivery. Gmail: create an App Password (Google Account ->
# Security -> 2-Step Verification -> App passwords) and use it as SMTP_PASS.
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
# SMTP_PORT=465
# SMTP_USER=you@gmail.com
# SMTP_PASS=your-16-char-app-password
# MAIL_FROM=Sakhi <you@gmail.com>
# Public base URL of the CLIENT, used to build links in emails.
# APP_BASE_URL=http://localhost:5173
